The race answers every pad button its card names.

`patches/0002` translates pad buttons to keys for screens that register no joystick
handler of their own. The race registers `RacingJoyButton`, so that translation never
ran there, and `RacingJoyButton` acted on Cross alone: mid-race, pause, trick and quit
had no button, although the help card (`patches/0005`) listed them.

`RacingJoyButton` now maps them itself: Cross and R1 charge and jump, L1 is the trick
modifier, Options pauses and Circle quits the race - the last two through `RacingKeys`,
so they do exactly what `p` and Escape do. The help card is corrected to match, lists
the left stick, and says Circle (the only button that leaves it) rather than "any key".

It also fixes two comments in `winsys.cpp` that had Square and Triangle swapped: the
backend reports Square as button 2 and Triangle as 3.

--- a/racing.cpp
+++ b/racing.cpp
@@ -109,11 +109,18 @@
 	}
 }
 
+// The race registers this handler, so `CWinsys::PadButtonKey`'s pad-to-key translation - which
+// only runs for screens without one - never reached it: mid-race only Cross did anything, and
+// pause, trick and quit had no button. Indices are the SDL backend's GameController order
+// (`oops-deps/sdl2/backend/SDL_prosperojoystick.c`).
 void RacingJoyButton (int button, int state) {
-	if (button == 0) {
-		key_charging = (bool) state;
-	} else if (button == 1) {
-//		key_charging = (bool) state;
+	switch (button) {
+		case 0:  key_charging = (bool) state; break;        // Cross - charge and jump
+		case 10: key_charging = (bool) state; break;        // R1 - the same
+		case 9:  trick_modifier = (bool) state; break;      // L1 - trick
+		case 6:  if (state) RacingKeys (SDLK_p, false, false, 0, 0); break;      // Options
+		case 1:  if (state) RacingKeys (SDLK_ESCAPE, false, false, 0, 0); break; // Circle
+		default: break;
 	}
 }
 
--- a/winsys.cpp
+++ b/winsys.cpp
@@ -433,15 +433,15 @@
 // press arrives at exactly the handler the same keypress would, and no screen's own code changes.
 //
 // The mapping is the console's convention rather than SDL's button order:
-//   cross -> Return, circle -> Escape, triangle -> Tab, square -> Backspace
+//   cross -> Return, circle -> Escape, square -> Tab, triangle -> Backspace
 // and the D-pad hat to the four arrows. `PROSPERO_BTN_*` in the SDL backend puts cross at 0 and
 // circle at 1, which is SDL_GameController order.
 unsigned int CWinsys::PadButtonKey (int button) {
 	switch (button) {
 		case 0: return SDLK_RETURN;     // cross
 		case 1: return SDLK_ESCAPE;     // circle
-		case 2: return SDLK_TAB;        // triangle
-		case 3: return SDLK_BACKSPACE;  // square
+		case 2: return SDLK_TAB;        // square
+		case 3: return SDLK_BACKSPACE;  // triangle
 
 		// **The race needs more than the menus do, and had none of it.**
 		//
